-- title:
--   Online randomized algorithms (Definition 2.9)
-- statement:
--   A randomized algorithm $\mathcal A:(\mathbb R^{d\times d})^N\times\Omega\to\{-1,1\}^N$ takes inputs $A_1,\dots,A_N$ and a random seed $\omega\in\Omega$ that is independent of the input. It is **online** if, for every $1\le j\le N$, the $j$-th sign
--
--   $$\sigma_j:=\mathcal A(A_1,\dots,A_N,\omega)_j\in\{-1,1\}$$
--
--   is measurable with respect to the $\sigma$-field generated by $(A_1,\dots,A_j,\omega)$. In particular, for fixed $\omega$, $\sigma_j$ is a function of $A_1,\dots,A_j$ only.
--
--   Algorithm 2 (Frobenius-greedy) is online, and Theorem 2.10 is a lower bound for this class.
--
--   **Formalization Note** Measurability with respect to $\sigma(A_1,\dots,A_j,\omega)$ is encoded as two conditions: $\sigma_j$ is jointly measurable in $(A,\omega)$, and it does not change when the inputs after $j$ change. On the product space the two together are equivalent to the paper's condition. Indices are 0-based, and the codomain $\{-1,1\}$ is recorded.
-- source:
--   Kızıldağ, Li, Stable and Online Algorithms for Random Matrix Discrepancy, arXiv:2610.01591 (preprint; held file sha256 889af7fc2595be5c653c6b477e70fa5898c7b743d8720cda44ca99761d629a13), §2.3, p.15 (PDF p.17), Definition 2.9

import Mathlib

namespace RandMatDisc.Supplement

/-- **Definition 2.9 (online algorithm)**, p.15 (PDF p.17) of Kızıldağ, Li, *Stable and Online
Algorithms for Random Matrix Discrepancy*, arXiv:2610.01591 (held file sha256 889af7fc…):

"A randomized algorithm A : ℝ^{d×d} ⋯ × ℝ^{d×d} × Ω → {−1, 1}ᴺ with input A₁, …, A_N and random
seed ω ∈ Ω independent of the input is online if for every 1 ≤ j ≤ N, σ_j := A(A₁, …, A_N, ω)_j
∈ {−1, 1} is measurable with respect to the σ-field generated by (A₁, …, A_j, ω). That is, for
fixed ω ∈ Ω, σ_j is a function of A₁, …, A_j only."

**Formalization Note.** Inputs are arrays `Fin d → Fin d → ℝ` (as for the published `goe`),
indices are 0-based. Measurability with respect to `σ(A₁, …, A_j, ω)` is encoded as: `σ_j` is
jointly measurable in `(A, ω)` and does not change when the inputs after `j` change. On the
product space these two together are equivalent to measurability with respect to
`σ(A₁, …, A_j, ω)` (compose with the map that resets the later inputs to `0`). -/
def IsOnline {d N : ℕ} {Ω : Type} [MeasurableSpace Ω]
    (alg : (Fin N → Fin d → Fin d → ℝ) → Ω → Fin N → ℝ) : Prop :=
  (∀ A ω j, alg A ω j = 1 ∨ alg A ω j = -1) ∧
  (∀ j, Measurable fun p : (Fin N → Fin d → Fin d → ℝ) × Ω => alg p.1 p.2 j) ∧
  ∀ j A A' ω, (∀ i, i ≤ j → A i = A' i) → alg A ω j = alg A' ω j

end RandMatDisc.Supplement


